{"from": "text:josephus-ant-12-316-325", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-22-feast-dedication", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: after the rededication Judas 'made it a law for their posterity' to keep an eight-day festival for the restoration of the temple worship, 'and from that time to this we celebrate this festival, and call it Lights' - the same festival, under another name, kept into Josephus's own day; he gives no month for it."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-22-feast-dedication", "grade": "B", "note": "The Second Temple stood in Jerusalem as the festival's focal site through the 30s CE; it attests the 'at Jerusalem' setting of the Dedication, not the festival's date."}
{"from": "observation:sheepfold-dry-stone", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-01-02-sheepfold-door", "grade": "C", "note": "'Most structures today referred to as sheepfolds are ancient dry stone semicircles' - walled sheep enclosures of great antiquity with restricted access, the shepherd economy of the fold; the observation is general (its examples are British) and says nothing of a single door or a gatekeeper."}
{"from": "observation:sheep-flock-behavior", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-03-05-shepherd-voice", "grade": "C", "note": "Sheep have 'a strong tendency to follow' their leader, and 'shepherds have often trained a wether... to lead the flock on voice command' - voice-led flock management is real husbandry; this is general ethology, not 1st-century Judea, and it attests the custom, not that the sheep followed Jesus."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-220-221", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-23-solomons-porch", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: Agrippa's workmen were persuaded 'to rebuild the eastern cloisters... This was the work of king Solomon' - a colonnade of Herod's temple traditionally ascribed to Solomon, the same institution John 10:23 names; Josephus confirms the portico and its attribution, not that Jesus walked there."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-23-solomons-porch", "grade": "B", "note": "Herod's temple stood in Jerusalem through the 30s CE with its porticoes; it attests the setting of walking 'in the temple', not this particular walk."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sanhedrin-7-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-33-blasphemy-stoning", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Sanhedrin 7:5: the blasphemer 'is not liable unless he utters the name' of God and curses it, with the judges tearing their garments - blasphemy tried as a capital charge before the court (Sanhedrin 7:1 lists stoning first among the four modes of execution). The Mishnah was redacted c. 200 CE and may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-33-blasphemy-stoning", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: in 62 CE Ananus 'assembled the sanhedrim of judges' and, on an accusation of breaking the law, delivered James 'to be stoned' - the sanhedrin invoking stoning for religious offence a generation later; it attests the practice, not the 30s charge of blasphemy."}
{"from": "observation:galilee-rainy-season", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-23-winter", "grade": "C", "note": "Israel's rains fall between November and March - the cold rainy season of the Judean highlands; 25 Kislev falls inside it, so the 'winter' of 10:22-23 is the region's wet season. It attests the season, not that any particular Dedication was wintry."}
{"from": "site:bethany-beyond-jordan", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-40-beyond-jordan-return", "grade": "B", "note": "Al-Maghtas on the east bank of the Jordan preserves Second Temple-period pools and buildings in a baptizing landscape - the place John 10:40 recalls as 'where John was baptizing at first'; it attests the place's use, not that Jesus made this journey."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-10-40-beyond-jordan-return", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: John 'commanded the Jews to exercise virtue... and so to come to baptism' and 'others also came together in crowds about him' - John's baptizing activity in the region, independent of the Gospels; it does not name Bethany beyond the Jordan and dates nothing of this return."}
{"from": "text:1-maccabees-4-52-59", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-10-22-feast-dedication", "note": "1 Maccabees 4:52-59: the altar dedicated eight days from 25 Chislev, and 'the days of the dedication of the altar' ordained 'from year to year' - the festival's biblical (deuterocanonical) origin; a cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "text:psalm-82", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-10-34-psalm-82-quote", "note": "Psalm 82:6: 'I said, you are gods, all of you are sons of the Most High' - the verse Jesus quotes as 'written in your law' in John 10:34; a biblical cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:jn-10-40-beyond-jordan-return", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-01-28-baptizing-bethany-beyond-jordan", "note": "John 10:40 revisits 'the place where John was baptizing at first' - 'at first' presupposes the Bethany-beyond-the-Jordan scene of John 1:28; the same location tradition, a later visit."}
{"from": "claim:jn-10-03-05-shepherd-voice", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-18-12-13-lost-sheep", "note": "The same shepherd imagery, not the same saying: Matthew's lost-sheep parable has the shepherd leave the ninety-nine on the mountains to seek the straying one; John's discourse has the shepherd call his own sheep by name and the sheep follow his voice."}
{"from": "claim:jn-10-03-05-shepherd-voice", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-09-36-sheep-shepherd", "note": "The same shepherd imagery, different sayings: Matthew's crowds are 'like sheep without a shepherd'; John's shepherd owns, calls and knows his sheep."}
{"from": "passage:jn-10-03", "type": "related_to", "to": "passage:lk-15-04", "note": "Shared shepherd imagery, different events: Luke 15:4 is the lost-sheep parable (which of you, losing one of a hundred...); John 10:3 is the gate-and-shepherd discourse - the same caring-shepherd image, not the same parable."}
{"from": "passage:jn-10-04", "type": "related_to", "to": "passage:mt-18-12", "note": "Shared shepherd imagery, different events: Matthew 18:12 is the lost-sheep parable on the mountains; John 10:4 is the discourse about the shepherd going before his sheep - the same image, not the same parable."}
